Release procedure:
 - update version number in README and slib.init
 - update date in README


GC

 - weak refs?
 - smaller custom gc module
   - much less code, hopefully
   - combine object tags with allocator overhead
   - special representations for the most common structures

C Friendliness

 - extensible type system
   - try to keep 1-word overhead per object
   - still fast, understandable to gc
 - mechanism to add new primitive procs
 - make all scheme procs available
   - C name-mangling
   - conventions for types of args/return value
   - conventional namespace prefix
 - what about SWIG?
 - invoking interpreter from C
   - dealing with continuations...

Misc

 - er, should quasiquote always create fresh structure?
 - program-arguments in slib.init
 - what's (compiler) in slib.init?
 - iterative append
 - write-char needs to flush().
 - generic ports
 - file ports hold onto their filename
 - a make_string that takes address/count
 - fix the ``double control-d to end'' problem
 - it's dangerous having every atom not a number read as a symbol
 - handle stack overflow gracefully -- spill to heap
 - more efficient continuations
 - macros
 - source-location info optionally created by READ, used by error messages
 - make Object an opaque pointer type
 - how to get this working on 16-bit/64-bit systems?
 - autogenerate the reified_cont_code, etc.
 - combo of string_to_symbol and c_string might be handy
 - with-fooput-to-file
 - sigint catcher
 - we have `fast' and `debug' versions of the C source, but there
   should also be an in-between level of checking that's only 
   suspicious of the compiler.  Maybe.
 - review r4rs and check everything
 - use gmp for the full numeric tower

Error Handling

 - always pass on all primitive args to error handler
 - if this can get embedded in important programs like text editors,
   we need to make sure it always fails gracefully.  Like if we're 
   completely out of memory, fatal_error() doesn't cut it.  I'm not
   sure what does.
 - the code assumes it's impossible to have a vector or list length
   that won't fit in a fixnum.  Better make sure.
 - what about this:

-> (close-output-port (current-output-port))
Fatal error: Stack overflow

   You could make any recursive error invocation fatal, but I think
that's going too far.  Another solution in this case is to refuse to
close that port...


Module System

Threads

 - the error-trap interface currently uses global state...

Code Cleanup

 - you're sometimes using chars, sometimes unsigned chars...
 - similarly with Fixnum -- and int isn't necessarily 32 bits
 - stop assuming ints and longs are the same
 - comments and overview
 - vm
   - break into modules?
 - compiler/library

Debugger

 - @error should be a byte-op, so it can be inlined, so that we don't
   normally have a tail call to the @error procedure, losing the context
 - nested break-levels
 - inspect arbitrary structures
 - eval an expression
 - better env display
 - have tracebacks show args
 - print-width, print-depth settings
 - a (break) procedure to go with (error)
 - tracing, stepping, advising?
 - source code info
 - reverse execution, algorithmic debugging (dream on)

Profiling

 - for internals
 - for user
 - see if Boehm gc gives stats

Efficiency

 - fasl format
   - collect symbols at the beginning
   - replace B (boolean) tag with T/F
   - replace for startup with a static heap?

 - vm code
   - tune the Boehm gc interface
   - keep top of stack in a register
   - increment pc just once per instruction
   - use pointers instead of indices for stack_ptr, etc?
   - combine adjacent check_type's?
   - don't pop off the last argument in prim instructions;
     use set_top() instead, and drop() on error or callout.

 - vm architecture
   - make READ into a primitive
   - explicit stack overflow checking in extend-env, etc.
   - specialize %varref for 0 0, 0 1, etc.
   - specialize %lit for common operands
     - such as -1 0 1 2 #t #f () 0.0 "" #!unspecified #()
   - one-byte primitive invocations
   - get rid of %save, use a %call and %tail-call instead
     - be careful about how that affects stack inspection
       for debugging & error recovery
   - 1-byte jump offsets
   - constants vector should be a subrange of the code vector,
     instead of separate.
   - %stack-ref (may obviate the above %varref optimization)
     - also needs arg-count checking without extending the env,
       and more compiler support
   - auto-combine/specialize ops by static profile a la Proebsting
   - share constants vectors?
   - share closures?
   - instructions for letrec?
   - explicit typechecks?
   - primitives to help speed up running the compiler...
   - combine invoke and extend-foo-env
   - avoid argcount checking in calls to known procs

 - compiler code generated
   - optimize out ((lambda () ...))
   - optimize ((lambda (arg*) ...) exp*)
     - do we need new opcodes, or is extend-normal-env okay?
       Hm, we do.
   - stack-allocate environments sometimes (see %stack-ref above)
   - inline functions
   - complete rewrite.  twobit looks nice.

 - compile-time efficiency
   - use an accumulator in parsing definitions
   - macroexpansion by calling code-gen functions instead of rewriting
